Thanks, that makes sense. I can see why the original validation was introduced as a safeguard to ensure we didn't accidentally process data from an outdated or incorrect CDA epoch following a redeployment, infrastructure change, or recovery activity. I think there is still value in retaining that validation, but instead of failing the job, automatically resolving the latest epoch would make the process more resilient. It would also be useful to understand what triggers these epoch changes on the Guidewire side (e.g. redeployment, infrastructure changes, recovery activities, etc.) so we know whether they are expected operational events or something that requires investigation.


A better approach would be:

Query Guidewire for the latest epoch at the start of every run.
If it differs from the cached/configured value:
Log that the epoch changed.
Update the cached value.
Continue processing.
Raise an informational alert so the team knows Guidewire changed the epoch.

This preserves the original safety objective while avoiding unnecessary pipeline failures.

So Paul's original design wasn't wrong—it reflected a defensive engineering principle: never continue processing if you're unsure you're reading from the correct source.
